[R0309] year=2026 | venue=PLOS ONE | type=Article
TITLE: Advanced persistent threat detection through multi-modal behavioral analysis
KEYWORDS: Algorithms; Data Analytics; Humans; Machine Learning; Pattern Recognition, Automated; adult; aged; animal hunting; article; controlled study; cyberattack; data analysis; false positive result; human; language model; large language model; machine learning; major clinical study; open access publishing; pattern recognition; simulation; surgical planning system; algorithm; automated pattern recognition; data analytics; machine learning; procedures
ABSTRACT: Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) represent sophisticated cyberattacks characterized by stealth, persistence, and evasion of traditional detection mechanisms. We observed that APT behaviors during lateral movement and data exfiltration share notable similarities with insider threat activities, leading us to explore cross-domain learning opportunities. This paper introduces a novel machine learning approach leveraging the CERT Insider Threat Dataset to simulate and detect APT behaviors through AI-augmented analytics. Our methodology integrates multi-modal data analysis, language model-driven behavioral understanding, and advanced machine learning to create realistic APT simulations from insider threat data. We developed three key technical components: a multi-agent language model architecture for log analysis, temporal sequence modeling for behavioral pattern recognition, and deep evidential clustering for uncertainty-aware threat detection that reduces false positives. Our research contributes four advances: a novel methodology for simulating APT patterns using insider threat data, an AI-enhanced multi-modal approach processing structured logs and communications, superior performance compared to existing methods, and practical deployment guidelines for enterprise environments. Experimental results achieved 96.3% detection accuracy while reducing false positives by 42% compared to state-of-the-art methods. Our system successfully simulates realistic APT scenarios across attack stages while providing interpretable explanations through natural language generation. The integration of large language models enables sophisticated analysis of unstructured data sources, offering contextual understanding beyond traditional approaches. This research addresses a critical gap for organizations seeking enhanced APT detection without extensive APT-specific training data. Our approach’s ability to learn from insider threat patterns while maintaining high accuracy makes it valuable for enterprise security operations and threat hunting teams facing resource constraints. © 2026 Adel Alshamrani. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original author and source are credited.

[R0310] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on AI Engineering and Innovations, AIEI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: LMDAHoney: Agentic-Language Model Driven Adaptive Honeypot for Advanced Cyber Deception
KEYWORDS: Adaptive Defense; Agentic AI; Autonomous Agents; Cyber Deception; Honeypot; Large Language Model; Threat Intelligence; Artificial intelligence; Information systems; Intelligent agents; Malware; Network security; Adaptive defense; Agentic AI; Cybe deception; Cyber-attacks; Honeypots; Language model; Large language model; Model-driven; Response patterns; Threat intelligence; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: Honeypots are widely used tools for proactive detection of cyberattacks. However, traditional honeypots have numerous limitations in providing a real-Time, deceptive environment for cyber attackers. The paper proposes a novel intelligent honeypot, LMDAHoney, that provides realistic engagements for attackers for extended periods through a series of prompt engineering techniques. It provides a shell-like framework with contextual capabilities, including adaptive deception, agents in the form of interactions, and an analytics (OIDC) module that provides a practical environment and logs all activities performed by cybercriminals. The request-response patterns in a typical shell-like environment are explored, and the LLM is trained to improve engagement. The Llama2-LLM model is leveraged and fine-Tuned to simulate the request-response patterns observed in real-world interactions with attackers. The proposed model is the first of its kind to integrate modern AI technologies into a cyber deception system, thereby enhancing the security posture of digitally connected assets. The proposed method is deployed in a real-world network. The experimental results show a precision-match accuracy of 88% and a similarity score of 90%, indicating a substantial proportion of model-generated responses match realistic SQL commands and engage the attacker for longer, thereby enabling the detection of advanced cyberattacks, including malware, botnets, and advanced persistent threats (APTs). © 2026 IEEE.

[R0311] year=2026 | venue=Engineering, Technology and Applied Science Research | type=Article
TITLE: Design and Empirical Evaluation of a Four-Layer AI Agent Architecture for Automated Web Application Security Testing
KEYWORDS: AI agent; autonomous security testing; deep learning; large language model; machine learning; OWASP; penetration testing; web application security
ABSTRACT: This study proposes a four-layer AI agent architecture for automating routine web security operations, integrating Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning with a hybrid Convolutional Neural Network-Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM) detection engine and implementing a Reasoning-Acting (ReAct) loop for autonomous testing with human-in-the-loop validation. The proposed architecture was empirically evaluated across 50 web applications sourced from OWASP WebGoat, DVWA, and custom-developed test environments over a six-month period. The experimental results demonstrate that the AI agent achieved an overall detection accuracy of 89.2% (95% CI: 86.4-92.0%), significantly outperforming traditional automated methods (67.4% accuracy, p < 0.001). Mean Time to Remediation (MTTR) decreased from 74.3 days to 28.5 days (61.6% reduction), while false positive rates decreased from 24.3% to 4.8%. According to these findings, AI agent-driven automation can substantially enhance the efficiency and reliability of web security testing. However, human expertise remains important for assessing complex vulnerabilities and detecting zero-day threats. © 2026 by the authors. All rights reserved.

[R0312] year=2023 | venue=2023 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security, CNS 2023 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Towards Evaluation and Understanding of Large Language Models for Cyber Operation Automation
KEYWORDS: autonomous cyber defence; benchmark dataset; cyber operational tasks; cyber security; natural language processing; Computational linguistics; Large dataset; Natural language processing systems; Petroleum reservoir evaluation; Risk assessment; Autonomous cybe defense; Benchmark datasets; Cybe operational task; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Language model; Language processing; Natural language processing; Natural languages; Operational tasks; Cybersecurity
ABSTRACT: Can foundational language models be useful in automating cybersecurity tasks? To address this open question, systematic and comprehensive evaluation of large language models (LLMs) across diverse cyber operational tasks (e.g., incident response, threat identification, forensic analysis, etc.), as well as understanding their risks and limitations, are crucial. A significant challenge lies in the absence of a standard benchmark dataset encompassing real-life cyber operational tasks that can be processed by LLMs. This paper tackles this challenge by conducting a preliminary study towards evaluation and understanding of LLMs for cyber operation automation. To that end, we first identify a list of defensive cyber operational tasks with increasing complexities and suggests the creation of new datasets to accomplish these tasks. Second, we review recent works leveraging LLMs for downstream cyber operational tasks to identify research gaps and open problems. Third, we propose a framework to understand and benchmark the cyber operational tasks to report potential solutions and research directions for the reliable evaluation of LLMs. Finally, this paper serves as an open call to the cybersecurity researchers and professionals to contribute to the development of an open-source evaluation framework paving the way for the trustworthy use of foundation models in cyber domain. © 2023 IEEE.

[R0313] year=2025 | venue=3rd International Conference on Emerging Research in Computational Science, ICERCS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Self-Auditing Large Language Models for Real-Time Intrusion Prediction in Encrypted Networks
KEYWORDS: anomaly detection; encrypted traffic analysis; flow-level intelligence; intrusion prediction; network forensics; privacy-preserving security; real-time IDS; Self-auditing LLMs; transformer models; uncertainty auditing; Computer crime; Computer forensics; Distributed computer systems; Electronic crime countermeasures; Forecasting; Intrusion detection; Network intrusion; Network security; Privacy-preserving techniques; Program processors; Throughput; Uncertainty analysis; Anomaly detection; Encrypted traffic; Encrypted traffic analyze; Flow-level; Flow-level intelligence; Intrusion prediction; Privacy preserving; Privacy-preserving security; Real- time; Real-time IDS; Self-auditing LLM; Traffic analysis; Transformer modeling; Uncertainty; Uncertainty auditing; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: Encrypted traffic increasingly limits signature-based intrusion detection, creating a critical gap in real-time threat prediction for modern high-throughput networks. This study introduces a self-auditing large language model (SA-LLM) capable of autonomous consistency checking, temporal reasoning, and uncertainty-aware inference without decrypting payloads. The model processes flow-level statistical descriptors, TLS handshake metadata, and sequence-aligned autoencoder embeddings to generate sub-millisecond intrusion predictions. A dual-loop auditing mechanism dynamically recalibrates thresholds under load based on token uncertainty and reconstruction divergence. Experiments were conducted using CIC-Darknet2020, USTC-TFC2018, and a proprietary TLS dataset spanning 52 encrypted attack classes. SA-LLM achieved 96.8% accuracy, a 37% reduction in false positives, and a 4.2× throughput improvement over transformer baselines. Practical testing was performed in Visual Studio Code on a standard laptop (Intel i7-12700H CPU, 16 GB RAM, RTX 3050 GPU), confirming stable inference performance at 9.6k flows/s in local mode with only 14% GPU utilization. Distributed edge deployment further scaled throughput to 18.4k flows/s with minimal overhead. These results validate the SA-LLM as a privacy-preserving, encryption-agnostic, and computationally efficient solution for real-time intrusion prediction across enterprise, industrial, and cloud infrastructures. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0314] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications, ICCSDFAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic AI Security in IoMT: A PRISMA-Guided Scoping Review and Layered Threat-Defense Taxonomy
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; cybersecurity; Internet of Medical Things; LLM agents; medical-device security; PRISMA-ScR; prompt injection; threat modeling; Anomaly detection; Autonomous agents; Biomedical equipment; Cybersecurity; Information use; Intrusion detection; Network security; Patient monitoring; Prisms; Agentic AI; Cyber security; Internet of medical thing; LLM agent; Medical Devices; Medical-device security; PRISMA-ScR; Prompt injection; Scoping review; Threat modeling; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: The Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) enables continuous patient monitoring and clinical connectivity but substantially expands the healthcare cybersecurity attack surface. AI/ML is frequently proposed as a defense mechanism in IoMT literature, addressing intrusion detection, anomaly detection, authentication, privacy, and secure communication. Emerging Agentic AI integration may introduce a distinct risk layer through external retrieval, long-term memory, dynamic tool use, prompt-driven behavior, autonomous action, and multi-agent coordination. Because direct empirical evidence from live Agentic IoMT deployments remains limited, we conducted a PRISMA-ScR-guided scoping review to map anticipated vulnerabilities. The final corpus contained 19 academic sources plus four standards, frameworks, and guidance documents identified through a parallel targeted search, totaling 23 core sources. Findings indicate that AI/ML is primarily positioned defensively in IoMT literature, whereas agentic capabilities introduce transferred risks from broader LLM-agent security domains. We contribute a seven-layer threat-defense taxonomy and a focused research agenda for empirical validation rather than claims of real-world attack prevalence. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0315] year=2024 | venue=AutonomousCyber 2024 - Proceedings of the Workshop on Autonomous Cybersecurity, Co-Located with: CCS 2024 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: PenHeal: A Two-Stage LLM Framework for Automated Pentesting and Optimal Remediation
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity Automation; LLMs; Penetration Testing; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Vulnerability Remediation; Remediation; Cyber security; Cybersecurity automation; Language model; Large language model; Model-based penetration testing; Modelling framework; Penetration testing; Remediation strategies; Retrieval-augmented generation; Vulnerability remediations; Integration testing
ABSTRACT: Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown significant potential in enhancing cybersecurity defenses against sophisticated threats. LLM-based penetration testing is an essential step in automating system security evaluations by identifying vulnerabilities. Remediation, the subsequent crucial step, addresses these discovered vulnerabilities. Since details about vulnerabilities, exploitation methods, and software versions offer crucial insights into system weaknesses, integrating penetration testing with vulnerability remediation into a cohesive system has become both intuitive and necessary. This paper introduces PenHeal, a two-stage LLM-based framework designed to autonomously identify and mitigate security vulnerabilities. The framework integrates two LLM-enabled components: the Pentest Module, which detects multiple vulnerabilities within a system, and the Remediation Module, which recommends optimal remediation strategies. The integration is facilitated through Counterfactual Prompting and an Instructor module that guides the LLMs using external knowledge to explore multiple potential attack paths effectively. Our experimental results demonstrate that PenHeal not only automates the identification and remediation of vulnerabilities but also significantly improves vulnerability coverage by 31%, increases the effectiveness of remediation strategies by 32%, and reduces the associated costs by 46% compared to baseline models. These outcomes highlight the trans-formative potential of LLMs in reshaping cybersecurity practices, offering an innovative solution to defend against cyber threats. © 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

[R0316] year=2025 | venue=2025 3rd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Blockchain, and Internet of Things, AIBThings 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: NetMoniAI: An Agentic AI Framework for Network Security & Monitoring
