ABSTRACT: In this paper, we present NetMoniAI, an agentic AI framework for automated network monitoring and security that integrates decentralized analysis with lightweight centralized coordination. The framework consists of two layers: autonomous micro-agents at each node perform local traffic analysis and anomaly detection. A central controller aggregates insights across nodes to identify coordinated attacks and maintain systemwide situational awareness. We evaluated NetMoniAI on both a local micro-testbed and through NS-3 simulations. The results confirm that the two-tier agentic AI design improves response time and maintains accuracy while avoiding duplicate reporting, thereby reducing redundant alerts. In addition, the framework demonstrates scalability across diverse deployment scenarios. The complete framework is available as open source for adoption and reproducibility. This enables researchers and practitioners to replicate, validate, and extend the framework across various network environments and threat scenarios. Github link: https://github.com/pzambare3/NetMoniAI. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0317] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Neural Networks | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Incorporating Distributed Invariants in Autonomous Cybersecurity Knowledge Graphs: A Scalable Approach Using GNNs and LLMs
KEYWORDS: Argumentation-based Knowledge Graphs (ACKGs); Cybersecurity; Graph Neural Networks (GNN); Large Language Models (LLM); Local Invariants; Microservices; Cybersecurity; Argumentation-based knowledge graph (autonomous cybersecurity knowledge graph); Cyber security; Graph neural network; Graph neural networks; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Local invariant; Microservice; Neural networks
ABSTRACT: This paper presents a novel methodology to enhance Autonomous Cybersecurity Knowledge Graphs (ACKGs) by incorporating distributed invariants, ensuring robust consistency in data integrity, access control, and threat detection. The proposed framework integrates Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and Large Language Models (LLMs), facilitating real-time validation, automated threat mitigation, and continuous system updates as evolving threats are detected. By embedding these invariants into the cybersecurity architecture, the approach offers a scalable, dynamic, and self-sustaining solution, significantly improving the resilience, adaptability, and operational efficiency of cybersecurity systems in complex, large-scale environments. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0318] year=2026 | venue=2026 6th International Symposium on Computer Technology and Information Science, ISCTIS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: MAPT: Memory-Augmented Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; LLM agents; Penetration testing; Computation theory; High level synthesis; Intelligent agents; Knowledge based systems; Network security; Cyber security; Hacker attacks; Language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Model-based OPC; Multiagent framework; Penetration testing; Slight variant; Subgoals; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing simulates hacker attacks to find vulnerabilities in information systems. Recent Large Language Model (LLM)-based pentesting systems show encouraging results on benchmarks, but their behavior in real deployment is hard to trust due to three intertwined problems: (1) their plans often contradict themselves within a single session, (2) agents tend to retry slight variants of failed commands instead of trying something new, and (3) the same prompt on the same target can lead to very different outcomes from one run to the next. To address these problems, we present MAPT, a memory-augmented multi-agent framework for automated penetration testing. MAPT runs agents in a plan-verify-act-reflect loop, built around three core components. The Strategic Planner compiles high-level objectives into a type-checked attack tree, where every leaf is a sub-goal that can be verified by a regex or a command exit code, so that each action is grounded in a structured plan rather than improvised mid-session. A Dual-Memory Module holds past trajectories on one side and a knowledge base of exploitation patterns on the other; this lets the agent recall what has already failed and pull in the right primitive for the current situation, instead of cycling through minor variants of the same failed command. A dedicated Critic agent reviews each command before it runs and labels each outcome afterwards, using a small fixed label set so that its decisions vary much less from run to run. In addition, a lightweight Tactical Executor deterministically translates each leaf sub-goal into a shell command, isolating strategic reasoning from low-level synthesis. Extensive experiments on AutoPenBench and NYU CTF Bench show that MAPT outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in both task success rate and cross-run stability. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0319] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics and Security | type=Article
TITLE: AutoPT: How Far Are We from the Fully Automated Web Penetration Testing?
KEYWORDS: AI Agent; Automation; Large Language Model; Web Penetration Testing; Ability testing; Artificial intelligence; Benchmarking; Intelligent agents; Network security; AI agent; Automated agents; End to end; Fully automated; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; State-machine; Web penetration testing; WEB security; Automation
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is essential for ensuring Web security by identifying and mitigating vulnerabilities in advance, and the rapid progress of large language models (LLMs) shows great potential to revolutionize this process through intelligent, automated agents. In this work, we establish a comprehensive end-to-end penetration testing benchmark using a real-world penetration testing environment to explore the capabilities of LLM-based agents in this domain. Our results reveal that the agents are familiar to procedures of penetration testing tasks, but they still face limitations in generating accurate commands and executing complete processes. Accordingly, we summarize the current challenges, including the difficulty of maintaining the entire message history and the tendency for the agent to become stuck. Based on the above insights, we propose a Penetration testing State Machine (PSM) that utilizes the Finite State Machine (FSM) methodology to address these limitations. Then, we introduce AutoPT, an automated penetration testing agent based on the principle of PSM driven by LLMs, which utilizes the inherent inference ability of LLM and the constraint framework of state machines. Our evaluation results show that AutoPT outperforms the the ReAct-based baseline and improves the task completion rate from 22% to 41% on the benchmark target. Compared with the baseline and manual work, AutoPT also reduces time and economic costs further. In general, our AutoPT has facilitated the development of automated penetration testing and bring new findings and insights for both academia and industry. © IEEE. 2005-2012 IEEE.

[R0320] year=2025 | venue=EMNLP 2025 - 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, Proceedings of the Conference | type=Conference paper
TITLE: From Capabilities to Performance: Evaluating Key Functional Properties of LLM Architectures in Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Computational linguistics; Coordination reactions; Memory architecture; Multi agent systems; Natural language processing systems; Comprehensive evaluation; Empirical performance; Functional properties; Language model; Model-based OPC; Modeling architecture; Modular designs; Multisteps; Penetration testing; Performance evaluating; Agents
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) have been explored for automating or enhancing penetration testing tasks, but their effectiveness and reliability across diverse attack phases remain open questions. This study presents a comprehensive evaluation of multiple LLM-based agents, ranging from singular to modular designs, across realistic penetration testing scenarios, analyzing their empirical performance and recurring failure patterns. We further investigate the impact of core functional capabilities on agent success, operationalized through five targeted augmentations: Global Context Memory (GCM), Inter-Agent Messaging (IAM), Context-Conditioned Invocation (CCI), Adaptive Planning (AP), and Real-Time Monitoring (RTM). These interventions respectively support the capabilities of Context Coherence & Retention, Inter-Component Coordination & State Management, Tool Usage Accuracy & Selective Execution, Multi-Step Strategic Planning & Error Detection & Recovery, and Real-Time Dynamic Responsiveness. Our findings reveal that while some architectures natively exhibit select properties, targeted augmentations significantly enhance modular agent performance-particularly in complex, multi-step, and real-time penetration testing scenarios. © 2025 Association for Computational Linguistics.

[R0321] year=2025 | venue=2025 5th International Conference on Advancement in Electronics and Communication Engineering, AECE 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Security Risks in AI-Generated Code: A Comparative Analysis of Industry and Academic Benchmarks
KEYWORDS: AI-Generated source Code; Code Safety; Dynamic Security Analysis; Large Language Models (LLMs); Static Security Analysis; Accident prevention; Benchmarking; Black-box testing; Codes (symbols); Computer programming languages; Computer systems programming; Dynamic analysis; Formal verification; Network security; Security systems; Static analysis; AI-generated source code; Code safety; Codegeneration; Comparative analyzes; Dynamic security analysis; Language model; Large language model; Security risks; Source codes; Static security analysis; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: In today's era of Generative AI, the Large Language Model (LLMs)applications are largely used to generate functioning source code. These applications simply does not require any programming skill except a prompt. However, the LLM generated codes are prone to safety and efficiency concerns. A common safety concerns in popular programming languages source code like Java, C++ and C are SQL injection, pointer dereferencing, improper input validation, etc. There are several academic studies and industry approaches to enable a strong and safe AI code generation such as prompt inversion, formal verification, and many more. In this paper, we analyze both the industry and academic approach to reduce and fix the threats over AI generated code and discussed key findings. The platform like Veracode that uses static analysis, dynamic testing and the platform CrowdStrike working to develop self-learning, multi-agent AI systems that employ Red Teaming capabilities. The academic benchmark like CodeLMSec that proposed black-box vulnerability probing framework, FormAI-v2 applies a large-scale formal verification for C language vulnerabilities, and the CodeSecEval benchmark that is based on prompt inversion to evaluate secure code generation and repair. Our studies on the mentioned benchmark highlights that the currently LLM generated source code persists vulnerabilities due to lack of benchmark dataset. The unsafe code fixes are approached in one direction only, either based on static analysis or dynamic analysis. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0322] year=2025 | venue=Applied Sciences (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: CurriculumPT: LLM-Based Multi-Agent Autonomous Penetration Testing with Curriculum-Guided Task Scheduling
KEYWORDS: autonomous driving security; curriculum learning; intelligent transportation systems; large language models; multi-agent system; penetration testing; sensor-based cyberphysical systems; Accident prevention; Autonomous agents; Autonomous vehicles; Complex networks; Coordination reactions; Embedded systems; Intelligent agents; Intelligent systems; Intelligent vehicle highway systems; Knowledge based systems; Knowledge management; Knowledge transfer; Network security; Traffic control; Autonomous driving; Autonomous driving security; Curriculum learning; Cyber-physical systems; Intelligent transportation systems; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Penetration testing; Sensor-based cyberphysical system; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: While autonomous driving systems and intelligent transportation infrastructures become increasingly software-defined and network-connected, ensuring their cybersecurity has become a critical component of traffic safety. Large language models (LLMs) have recently shown promise in automating aspects of penetration testing, yet most existing approaches remain limited to simple, single-step exploits. They struggle to handle complex, multi-stage vulnerabilities that demand precise coordination, contextual reasoning, and knowledge reuse. This is particularly problematic in safety-critical domains, such as autonomous vehicles, where subtle software flaws can cascade across interdependent subsystems. In this work, we present CurriculumPT, a novel LLM-based penetration testing framework specifically designed for the security of intelligent systems. CurriculumPT combines curriculum learning and a multi-agent system to enable LLM agents to progressively acquire and apply exploitation skills across common vulnerabilities and exposures-based tasks. Through a structured progression from simple to complex vulnerabilities, agents build and refine an experience knowledge base that supports generalization to new attack surfaces without requiring model fine-tuning. We evaluate CurriculumPT on 15 real-world vulnerabilities scenarios and demonstrate that it outperforms three state-of-the-art baselines by up to 18 percentage points in exploit success rate, while achieving superior efficiency in execution time and resource usage. Our results confirm that CurriculumPT is capable of autonomous, scalable penetration testing and knowledge transfer, laying the groundwork for intelligent security auditing of modern autonomous driving systems and other cyberphysical transportation platforms. © 2025 by the authors.

[R0323] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of Machine Learning Research | type=Conference paper
TITLE: ScamAgents: How AI Agents Can Simulate Human-Level Scam Calls
KEYWORDS: AI Red Teaming; Autonomous Agents; Conversational AI; LLM Safety and Guardrail Evasion; Scam Call Simulation; Social Engineering Attacks; Computational linguistics; Guard rails; Human computer interaction; Multi agent systems; Network security; Speech processing; AI red teaming; Conversational AI; Human levels; Language model; Large language model safety and guardrail evasion; Multi-turn; Red teaming; Scam call simulation; Social engineering; Social engineering attack; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated impressive fluency and reasoning capabilities, but their potential for misuse has raised growing concern. In this paper, we present ScamAgent, an autonomous multi-turn agent built on top of LLMs, capable of generating highly realistic scam call scripts that simulate real-world fraud scenarios. Unlike prior work focused on single-shot prompt misuse, ScamAgent maintains dialogue memory, adapts dynamically to simulated user responses, and employs deceptive persuasion strategies across conversational turns. We show that current LLM safety guardrails, including refusal mechanisms and content filters, are ineffective against such agent-based threats. Even models with strong prompt-level safeguards can be bypassed when prompts are decomposed, disguised, or delivered incrementally within an agent framework. We further demonstrate the transformation of scam scripts into lifelike voice calls using modern text-to-speech systems, completing a fully automated scam pipeline. Our findings highlight an urgent need for multi-turn safety auditing, agent-level control frameworks, and new methods to detect and disrupt conversational deception powered by generative AI. © 2025 S. Badhe.

[R0324] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Electronics, Computers and Artificial Intelligence, ECAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: InfraAgent: A Multi-Agent AI Framework for Predictive Deployment Failure Detection and Autonomous Remediation in Multi-Cloud Environments
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Autonomous Deployment; Capacity Forecasting; CI/CD Automation; Cloud Operations; DevSecOps; Infrastructure Management; Large Language Model Agents; Multi-Agent Systems; Predictive Remediation; Site Reliability Engineering; Temporal Graph Attention Networks; Automation; Autonomous agents; Distributed computer systems; Failure (mechanical); Forecasting; Intelligent agents; Network security; Outages; Remediation; Risk assessment; Safety engineering; Telemetering; Telemetering equipment; Agentic AI; Autonomous deployment; Capacity forecasting; Cloud operation; Continuous integration/continuous delivery automation; Continuous integrations; Devsecop; Infrastructure managements; Language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Multiagent systems (MASs); Predictive remediation; Reliability (engineering); Site reliability engineering; Temporal graph attention network; Temporal graphs; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Managing modern cloud-native infrastructure is, frankly, a problem that has grown faster than our tools for handling it. Continuous delivery pipelines, ephemeral containers, and sprawling multi-cloud Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) ecosystems create an operational surface that expands combinatorially with organisational scale and static rule-based monitoring simply cannot keep up. This paper presents InfraAgent, a five-agent agentic AI framework designed for predictive infrastructure management and autonomous deployment operations in heterogeneous multi-cloud settings. The framework brings together: a Temporal Graph Attention Network (T-GAN) agent that jointly models infrastructure dependency topology and multi-variate telemetry for deployment failure prediction across 1-hour, 6-hour, and 24-hour horizons; a Remediation Planning Agent (RPA) built on a Large Language Model (LLM) that generates context-aware IaC patches, rollout prescriptions, and rollback directives directly from risk scores; a Deployment Safety Agent (DSA) that enforces configurable pre-flight autonomy gates; a Capacity Forecasting Agent (CFA) built on a Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT) for probabilistic 24-hour to 72-hour demand forecasting; and a Continuous Operational Intelligence Orchestrator (COIO) that provides closed-loop governance, cryptographic auditability, and online model adaptation. We evaluate InfraAgent on 47,800 deployment events and 310,000 telemetry snapshots drawn from AWS, Azure, and GCP production environments. The results show a Mean Time to Detection of 4.3 minutes (an 85.2% reduction over rule-based baselines), a deployment failure prediction F1 of 0.931, a 76.8% reduction in production incidents per 100 Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) runs, and 91.4% capacity forecast accuracy at the 72-hour horizon, suggesting that coordinated agentic reasoning grounded in graph-temporal learning can meaningfully shift cloud operations from reactive firefighting toward continuous, self-correcting infrastructure assurance. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0325] year=2026 | venue=Computer Networks | type=Article
TITLE: Hierarchical multi-agent AI framework for cybersecurity in cyber–physical systems
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cyber–physical systems; eXplainable Artificial Intelligence; Intrusion detection systems; Network security; Computer architecture; Computer crime; Critical infrastructures; Cyber Physical System; Cybersecurity; Distributed computer systems; Embedded systems; Hierarchical systems; Intelligent agents; Intrusion detection; Multi agent systems; Network architecture; Public works; Safety engineering; Agentic AI; Cybe-physical systems; Cyber security; Cyber-physical systems; Explainable artificial intelligence; Intrusion Detection Systems; Multi agent; Networked cyber-physical systems; Networks security; Physical process; Network security
ABSTRACT: Cyber–Physical Systems (CPS) drive modern critical infrastructures by tightly integrating physical processes with computation and communication networks. This convergence exposes CPS to sophisticated cyber threats propagating across physical, control, and network layers, where stealthy and multi-stage attacks manifest through weak and distributed signals, challenging centralized intrusion detection systems and monolithic AI models that struggle to scale across heterogeneous subsystems and lack the interpretability required in safety-critical environments. This paper proposes a hierarchical multi-agent AI framework for automated cybersecurity assessment with human-understandable explanations in networked CPS. The framework dynamically instantiates a task-specific agent hierarchy from a natural language description of the target system, aligning the security analysis process with the underlying CPS architecture. Specialized agents perform fine-grained analysis of heterogeneous data sources, subsystem supervisors aggregate and contextualize local findings, and a deliberative round-table consensus mechanism enables cross-subsystem correlation for detecting coordinated and stealthy attacks. The framework is evaluated using four LLMs (Qwen 3 4B, Qwen 3 8B, Llama 3.1 8B, and Llama 3.3 70B) across three datasets (PicoDomain, CERT r5.2, and SWaT), achieving perfect recall for models with 8B parameters and above (i.e., zero missed attacks), while reaching an accuracy of 90.18% on PicoDomain, 92.77% on CERT r5.2, and 96.97% on SWaT. An ablation study confirms the effectiveness of the cross-subsystem consensus mechanism, demonstrating substantial precision gains when collaborative deliberation is enabled. Overall, this work establishes hierarchical multi-agent architectures as a scalable, interpretable, and structure-aware foundation for AI-driven cybersecurity in networked CPS. © 2026 The Authors

[R0326] year=2025 | venue=2025 28th International Conference on Computer and Information Technology, ICCIT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Code Poisoning Through Misleading Comments: Jailbreaking Large Language Models via Contextual Deception
KEYWORDS: Adversarial attack; AI safety; Code comments; Code poisoning; Contextual deception; Jailbreaking; Large language models (LLMs); LLM vulnerabilities; Artificial intelligence; Computer aided software engineering; Computer software; Malware; Safety engineering; AI safety; Code comment; Code poisoning; Contextual deception; Jailbreaking; Language model; Large language model; Large language model vulnerability; Malwares; Codes (symbols)
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) increasingly underpin everyday software tooling; yet, their deference to surrounding comments creates a subtle yet potent attack surface. We demonstrate a new jail-break technique that hides prohibited requests inside ostensibly educational or maintenance comments, inducing models to emit disallowed code and instructions despite alignment safeguards. To quantify the risk, we assemble a 200 prompt benchmark covering seven harm categories (e.g., physical, economic, malware) and five narrative frames (e.g., research simulation, penetration testing), each expressed as short Python snippets with carefully crafted deceptive annotations. A 3 × 3 factorial study probes three state-of-the-art LLMs like Gemini-2.0Flash, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-70B, and LLaMA-3.3-70BVersatile, evaluating 1,800 generations with automated rules plus expert adjudication for both 'harmfulness' and 'harm awareness'. Attacks succeed in 63% - 93.5% of cases; LLaMA-3.3-70B-Versatile proves most susceptible, while malware and illegal activity prompts achieve near-perfect bypass rates. More than half of successful outputs are produced 'harmful & unaware,' indicating that current safety layers frequently fail silently rather than refuse. The results call for comment-level auditing whenever LLMs are deployed in production code workflows. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0327] year=2025 | venue=Applied Sciences (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: PenQA: A Comprehensive Instructional Dataset for Enhancing Penetration Testing Capabilities in Language Models
KEYWORDS: cybersecurity; large language models; penetration testing; Cyber security; Domain specific; Evaluation metrics; Fine tuning; Language model; Large language model; Model domains; Modeling architecture; Penetration testing; Question-answer pairs
ABSTRACT: Large language models’ domain-specific capabilities can be enhanced through specialized datasets, yet constructing comprehensive cybersecurity datasets remains challenging due to the field’s multidisciplinary nature. We present PenQA, a novel instructional dataset for penetration testing that integrates theoretical and practical knowledge. Leveraging authoritative sources like MITRE ATT&CK™ and Metasploit, we employ online large language models to generate approximately 50,000 question–answer pairs.We demonstrate PenQA’s efficacy by fine-tuning language models with fewer than 10 billion parameters. Evaluation metrics, including the BLEU, ROUGE, and BERTScore, show significant improvements in the models’ penetration testing capabilities. PenQA is designed to be compatible with various model architectures and updatable as new techniques emerge. This work has implications for automated penetration testing tools, cybersecurity education, and decision support systems. The PenQA dataset is available in our GitHub repository. © 2025 by the authors.

[R0328] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of 5th International Conference on Ubiquitous Computing and Intelligent Information Systems, ICUIS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: LLM-Powered Deep Reinforcement Learning for Predictive Cyber Threat Mitigation
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Cyber Defense; Deep Reinforcement Learning; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent System; Proactive Security; Red-Blue Simulation; Computational methods; Deep learning; Deep reinforcement learning; Hybrid systems; Network security; Reinforcement learning; Semantics; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber threats; Cyber-defense; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Proactive security; Red-blue simulation; Reinforcement learnings; Threats mitigations; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Current cyber defense systems are mostly reactive. They only address threats after an initial compromise occurs. This creates a critical vulnerability window that advanced attackers can exploit. We propose a new proactive defense framework that combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) to help predict and prevent threats. Our hybrid system uses LLMs for strategic threat analysis and DRL for real-time tactical adjustments. We validated the system through competitive red-blue team simulations based on the MITRE ATT&CK framework, running 1,000 engagement rounds across five trials with 200 rounds each against adaptive LLM-guided attackers. The results show an 80% defensive win rate, a 55.7% attack detection rate, and a 34.2% average maximum system compromise, which is significantly below critical limits. The complete setup achieves an 80% win rate, representing a 21.6% improvement over isolated parts. Our semantic caching optimization shows a 62.8% hit rate with response times under 0.5 seconds, proving it is practical for Security Operations Center (SOC) deployment. These findings demonstrate that merging semantic reasoning with adaptive learning allows defenders to better anticipate and neutralize complex attacks than traditional reactive or rule-based methods. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0329] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 2nd Secure and Trustworthy Cyberinfrastructure for IoT and Microelectronics, SATC 2026 - Conference Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Benchmarking the Effectiveness of AI-Driven Red Teaming Across Safety-Aligned Language Models
KEYWORDS: adversarial prompting; AI safety; black-box attacks; evolutionary algorithms; jailbreak attacks; Large language models; model alignment; red teaming; safety evaluation; Artificial intelligence; Benchmarking; Iterative methods; Network security; Safety engineering; Adversarial prompting; AI safety; Black boxes; Black-box attack; Jailbreak attack; Language model; Large language model; Model alignment; Red teaming; Safety evaluations; Evolutionary algorithms
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) have advanced rapidly, yet even safety-aligned models remain vulnerable to adversarial prompts that bypass safeguards and induce harmful outputs. Conventional red teaming methods, including static testing and gradient-based attacks, are limited by either insufficient adaptability or restrictive access assumptions. In this work, we benchmark an autonomous AI-driven evolutionary red teaming system that iteratively generates, evaluates, and refines prompts to uncover safety vulnerabilities in 7-billion-parameter aligned LLMs. Using Qwen-7B-Instruct as a prompt-mutation agent, the AI-driven method achieves a 47.0 % method-level Attack Success Rate (ASR), matching the GCG baseline (47.0 %) and exceeding static prompting (33.5 \%). We evaluate four open-source models (Gemma-7B, Llama-2-7B-Chat, Vicuna-7B, Mistral-7B) using the AdvBench harmful behaviors benchmark and a dual-stage evaluation framework (Llama-Guard-3 classification and Beaver7B severity scoring), achieving a 0 % false positive rate on 200 benign prompts. Results show substantial cross-model robustness variation (14.7 % to 64.0 % overall ASR; 4.4x spread), indicating that safety performance is strongly model-dependent even at similar scale. These findings show that adaptive black-box red teaming is competitive with strong gradient-based baselines and useful for scalable safety auditing. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0330] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on AI-Driven Smart Systems and Ubiquitous Computing, ICAUC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Cloud Security Hardening using Generative AI and Reinforcement Learning in Zero Trust Architectures
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Systems; Cloud Security; Generative AI; Reinforcement Learning; Zero Trust Architecture; Architecture; Computer architecture; Machine learning; Network security; Security systems; Trusted computing; Artificial intelligence learning; Autonomous system; Cloud environments; Cloud securities; Generative AI; Reinforcement learnings; Security hardening; Security modeling; Self-directed; Zero trust architecture; Cloud security
ABSTRACT: Cloud environments get much more complex, the traditional security model is no longer equipped to handle such attacks. ZTA or zero trust architecture is often referred to as a security model for the mind, assuming that any and every system cannot be trusted. However, traditional ZTA implementations tend to be rather stable. This study proposes a framework that can develop a self-directed automated cloud security system through Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA), using Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) and Reinforcement Learning (RL). The system automatically responds to attacks by observing the behavior of the sub-systems. After observing it response, it can update and modify the attack policy in a real time. With GenAI to formulate the policies, and Reinforcement Learning (RL) for decision making through feedback, this system strengthens security posture, improves threat detection and enforces Zero Trust compliance. The system can respond to new threats and vulnerabilities. This makes the cloud infrastructures more resilient. The first assessment revealed that the proposed system is better than existing models in terms of flexibility, efficiency, and protection against cyber threats. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0331] year=2024 | venue=2024 IEEE 14th Annual Computing and Communication Workshop and Conference, CCWC 2024 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Using Natural Language Processing Tools to Infer Adversary Techniques and Tactics Under the Mitre ATT&CK Framework
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Decision making; Forecasting; Intrusion detection; Natural language processing systems; Cyber-defense; Intrusion Detection Systems; Language model; Language processing techniques; Natural interfaces; Natural Language Processing Tools; Natural languages; Network operations; Networks security; Operational impacts; Network security
ABSTRACT: This paper presents a novel approach to interpreting Intrusion Detection System (IDS) rules and predicting likely attacker Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) through the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) trained to associate IDS rules to TTPs in the MITRE ATT&CK framework. Our methodology focuses on automatically describing IDS rules to enhance explainability and provide a natural interface for exploring the potential operational impacts that an unmitigated attack may have on network security and operations. Using natural language processing techniques, we trained several models on a dataset of 972 labeled IDS rules, enabling it to generate descriptive narratives to aid in explaining individual rules and providing a foundation for predicting associated TTPs to aid in defensive cyber operations. We present our approach and initial results, discuss the potential implications in the broader context of cyber defense, and propose a conceptual extension where identified TTPs are used to predict and suggest proactive and reactive defense strategies that may be acted upon by human and autonomous defenders. We believe this and related work pave the way for more dynamic and informed decision-making processes in cyber defense, potentially transforming how Security Operations Centers (SOCs) and autonomous cyber defense systems operate. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0332] year=2026 | venue=CEUR Workshop Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: The Agentic Kill Chain: A Cognitive Threat Framework for Autonomous Agent Ecosystems
KEYWORDS: Agent Security; Agentic AI; Cyber Threat Modelling; Kill Chain; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent Systems; Web of Agents; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Chains; Cognitive systems; Crime; Ecosystems; Electric circuit breakers; Intelligent agents; Interoperability; Modeling languages; Network architecture; Network security; Semantic Web; Semantics; Agent security; Agentic AI; Cybe threat modeling; Cyber threats; Kill chain; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Threat modeling; Web of agent; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: The transition from the static Web of Documents to the dynamic Web of Agents represents a paradigm shift from “semantics-in-data” to “semantics-in-model,” where autonomous systems leverage Large Language Models to execute complex workflows. While emerging protocols like the Model Context Protocol and Agent-to-Agent standardize interoperability, they significantly expand the attack surface by introducing autonomous decision-making loops that bypass traditional network-centric defences. This paper introduces the Agentic Kill Chain, a novel threat modelling framework that maps the attack lifecycle to the cognitive Observe-Orient-Decide-Act loop of autonomous agents. We define a four-dimensional taxonomy of cognitive threats: Semantic Infection (perception compromise via context poisoning), Cognitive Compromise (reasoning corruption via logical bifurcations), Agency Propagation (lateral movement via identity inheritance), and Systemic Execution (irreversible impact via action laundering). We validate the framework through a forensic trace analysis of a multi-agent financial fraud scenario (‘The Poisoned Concierge’), demonstrating how cognitive exploits remain invisible to conventional security controls. Finally, we introduce a layered defensive architecture based on three paradigms (Context Auditing for perceptual integrity, Intent Verification for cognitive authorization, and Circuit Breakers for mechanical impact containment) to countermeasure the novel attack surfaces typical of Agentic AI. © 2026 Copyright for this paper by its authors.

[R0333] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 International Conference on Recent Innovation in Science Engineering and Technology, ICRISET 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) Guided Cyber Attack Detection and Classification in Autonomous Intelligent Cars
KEYWORDS: Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs); Deep Belief Networks (DBNs); Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS); Vehicle Ad Hoc Networks (VANETs); Automobile drivers; Autonomous vehicles; Convolutional neural networks; Cybersecurity; Deep neural networks; Highway traffic control; Intelligent systems; Intelligent vehicle highway systems; Learning systems; Motion planning; Motor transportation; Network security; Transportation routes; Vehicle to vehicle communications; Convolutional neural network; Cyber security; Cyber-attacks; Deep belief network; Deep belief networks; Intelligent transportation system; Intelligent transportation systems; Vehicle ad hoc network; Vehicle ad-hoc networks; Decision making
ABSTRACT: A reliable network solution is needed due to the rapid growth of autonomous vehicles and Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS). So, we developed a new Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) Guided Cyber Attack Detection and Classification in Autonomous Intelligent Cars (GAICAD) model meets these needs by integrating advanced deep learning, path planning, and GenAI-based cybersecurity within Vehicle Ad Hoc Networks (VANETs). GAICAD uses a hybrid deep learning technique combining CNNs and DBNs to better categorize and predict driving conditions, improving network security and vehicle communication. The A∗ path planning method to find optimal routes amid varying traffic. GenAI-driven cybersecurity also detects threats and anomalies in real-time. Simulation results show GAICAD with previous models, achieving a 96% data delivery rate and a minimal data leak rate of 2%. It maintains effective communication with an average delay of 320 ms, even in heavy traffic. GAICAD improves traffic management, cybersecurity, and real-time decision-making, supporting the development of safer autonomous vehicle systems in smart cities. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0334] year=2026 | venue=International Conference on the European Energy Market, EEM | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Collaborative Cyberattack Mitigation Framework for Energy Grids
KEYWORDS: Agent-based systems; cyberattack mitigation strategies; cybersecurity; energy grids; group decision-making; multi-agent negotiations; Anomaly detection; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Cost effectiveness; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Distributed computer systems; Energy policy; Information systems; Information use; Intelligent agents; Network security; Agent-based systems; Cyber security; Cyber-attacks; Cyberattack mitigation strategy; Energy grids; Energy markets; Group Decision Making; Group decision making process; Mitigation strategy; Multiagent negotiation; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity is becoming paramount in energy grids and markets. In line with these trends, this paper introduces a collaborative mitigation model for energy grids that builds on the group decision-making process for cyberattack mitigation. It first defines a multi-criteria decision model for the energy sector that considers energy-related criteria for the efficiency of mitigation strategies and the impacts of cyber threats. It then advances the process with a multi-agent protocol based on an integrative multi-lateral negotiation algorithm to automate group decision-making. Agents define mitigation strategies to address cyberattack vectors and vulnerabilities affecting assets within the energy grid. Based on consensus and penalty measures, agents seek an optimal cost-effective strategy that minimizes impact on all stakeholders, i.e., asset owners, grid operators, consumers, and producers. The core mitigation system is integrated with several external components for incident detection and vulnerability identification, including anomaly detection systems, common repositories, collaborative platforms, and large language models. The model is evaluated based on two use cases for the energy grid. First, we integrate it with the cooperative negotiation model for self-sustainable energy communities. Second, we apply it on diverse organizational levels to select mitigation countermeasures to strengthen the resilience of a SCADA-supervised energy network. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0335] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Service Operations and Logistics, and Informatics, SOLI | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Building a Domain-Specialized Large Language Model for Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Domain Adaption; Fine-Tuning; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Retrieval-Augment Generation; Artificial intelligence; Computational linguistics; Computational methods; Cybersecurity; Information systems; Information use; Cyber security; Domain adaptions; Domain specific; Fine tuning; Language model; Large language model; Open-source model; Penetration testing; Question-answer pairs; Retrieval-augment generation; Integration testing
ABSTRACT: This paper investigates the effectiveness of fine-tuning versus Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) in adapting Large Language Models (LLMs) for the penetration testing domain. Open-source models were fine-tuned using custom datasets composed of question-answer pairs derived from curated cybersecurity sources, web-crawled glossaries, and domain-specific literature. Model performance was evaluated using BERTScore metrics. Findings indicate that fine-tuned models often underperform their base counterparts, aligning with existing research. The integration of RAG was then implemented and demonstrated that it significantly improved precision, recall, and F1 scores, highlighting its value in enhancing the contextual accuracy and domain relevance of LLMs for penetration testing tasks. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0336] year=2026 | venue=2026 9th International Conference on Communication Engineering and Technology, ICCET 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: A Hierarchically Autonomous Multi-Agent Framework with Target-Centered DAG Memory for Long-Horizon Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Automated Penetration Testing; Graph-Based Memory; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent Systems; Autonomous agents; Computation theory; Distributed computer systems; Graph structures; Graphic methods; Hierarchical systems; Intelligent agents; Memory architecture; Acyclic graphs; Automated penetration testing; Graph-based; Graph-based memory; Language model; Large language model; Model-driven; Multiagent framework; Multiagent systems (MASs); Penetration testing; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Large language model (LLM)-driven autonomous red teaming still suffers from reasoning drift, context overload, and unstable cross-stage coordination in long-horizon penetration tasks. This paper presents a multi-agent collaborative decisionmaking framework for autonomous penetration testing. The framework follows a hierarchically autonomous architecture in which a Planner performs global goal decomposition and strategy orchestration, while reconnaissance and exploitation agents receive high-level objectives and independently conduct local analysis, action planning, and execution adjustment, instead of relying on fine-grained step-by-step instructions from the Planner. To address context-window limitations and attention dispersion over long attack chains, we further introduce an MCP-based directed acyclic graph (DAG) memory that organizes key observations, environment states, and causal dependencies accumulated during penetration testing into a traceable graph structure. Each agent retrieves target-centered memory slices around its current objective, enabling task-relevant context injection while suppressing irrelevant information. By coupling hierarchical autonomy with structured memory retrieval, the proposed framework strengthens strategic continuity, contextual relevance, and collaboration stability in long-running offensive workflows, providing a unified architectural foundation for scalable autonomous red-team systems. © 2026 IEEE.


